# Normalise line endings in the repository to LF.
* text=auto

# These are executed INSIDE Linux containers. A CRLF here makes the shebang
# read as "/bin/bash\r", the interpreter is not found, the Postgres init script
# fails, and the database container exits on startup taking the whole stack
# with it. Observed exactly that on 2026-08-11.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.sql  text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Same reasoning, one layer up. Every RUN in the Dockerfile continues across
# lines with a trailing backslash, and a CRLF checkout carries the \r into the
# shell inside the image. .dockerignore is read line by line by the daemon.
# Neither is ever opened by a Windows tool, so LF unconditionally.
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile    text eol=lf
.dockerignore   text eol=lf

# C# keeps LF in the working tree as well as in the repository, which text=auto
# alone does not do — it stores LF and hands Windows CRLF on checkout. That is
# invisible until a test compares a raw string literal against something the
# code built with "\n": the literal carries the checkout's line endings and the
# code does not. Fourteen FileRewriterTests fail that way on any Windows clone,
# and did, while passing for everyone whose working copy predated the checkout
# that converted them. Observed 2026-08-14. The alternative — normalising inside
# every assertion — makes each test carry a workaround for a repository setting.
*.cs   text eol=lf

# Windows-native scripts keep CRLF.
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf

# Never touch these.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.pdf  binary
